# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
euthydemus-learn.md:13: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.2 have nothing from it; euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.3 has "¬knew → ¬wise_know"; they differ when only "when they were learners they were wise (knowing the things in question)" is true, which the first allows
euthydemus-learn.md:13: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "when they were learners they were wise (knowing the things in question)" is true and "when they were learners they knew the things they were learning" is false. Is S7, "And were you wise then?", true then?
euthydemus-learn.md:15: argue/readings: S8 is read 3 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r2 has "¬knew → ¬wise_know"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.2 has "¬wise_know"; they differ when only "when they were learners they knew the things they were learning" and "when they were learners they were wise (knowing the things in question)" are true, which the first allows; euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it
euthydemus-learn.md:15: argue/answered: S8: answered no, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "when they were learners they knew the things they were learning" and "when they were learners they were wise (knowing the things in question)" are true. Is S8, "No, indeed, he said.", true then?
euthydemus-learn.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r2 has "learner ∧ unlearned → unlearned_learn" and "learner ∧ ¬wise_know → ¬wise_learn"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.3 have "learner ∧ unlearned → unlearned_learn ∧ ¬wise_learn" and "wise_apt_learned → wise_apt_learn ∧ ¬dull_learn"; they differ when only "Cleinias and the other boys were learners, taught by the grammar-master and the lyre-master", "when they were learners they were wise (knowing the things in question)", "when they were learners they were unlearned (ignorant)", "the unlearned (the ignorant) are the ones who learn" and "the wise (those who already know) are the ones who learn" are true, which the first allows
euthydemus-learn.md:25: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: euthydemus-learn.logic.r2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.2 have "unlearned_learn ∧ ¬wise_learn"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.3 has "wise_apt_learn ∧ ¬dull_learn"; they differ when only "the unlearned (the ignorant) are the ones who learn", "the wise (clever) are the learners" and "the unlearned (dull) are the learners" are true, which the first allows
euthydemus-learn.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: euthydemus-learn.logic.r2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.2 put it at S13, "Then the unlearned learn, and not the wise, Cleinias, as you imagine."; euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.3 at S17, "Then after all the wise are the learners and not the unlearned; and your last answer to Euthydemus was wrong."
euthydemus-learn.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S13, "Then the unlearned learn, and not the wise, Cleinias, as you imagine.", rather than S17, "Then after all the wise are the learners and not the unlearned; and your last answer to Euthydemus was wrong."?
euthydemus-learn.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if when the grammar-master dictated, it was the wise (clever) boys who learned the dictation, then (the wise (clever) are the learners and it is not so that the unlearned (dull) are the learners)"; euthydemus-learn.logic.r2 does not
euthydemus-learn.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.2 and euthydemus-learn.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if when the grammar-master dictated, it was the wise (clever) boys who learned the dictation, then (the wise (clever) are the learners and it is not so that the unlearned (dull) are the learners)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
